In order to see how this works precisely, let us first define suitable ladder operators:
$$
\begin{aligned}
\hat{T}_{ \pm} & \equiv \hat{T}_{1} \pm i \hat{T}_{2} \\
\hat{V}_{ \pm} & \equiv \hat{T}_{4} \pm i \hat{T}_{5} \\
\hat{U}_{ \pm} & \equiv \hat{T}_{6} \pm i \hat{T}_{7}
\end{aligned}
$$

The commutation relations for the ladder operators read:
$$
\begin{aligned}
{\left[\hat{T}_{3}, \hat{T}_{ \pm}\right] } & = \pm \hbar \hat{T}_{ \pm} \\
{\left[\hat{T}_{+}, \hat{T}_{-}\right] } & =2 \hbar \hat{T}_{3}
\end{aligned}
$$
These relations are the same as in the angular momentum algebra for $\hat{L}^{z}$ and the ladder operators $\hat{L}_{ \pm}$.

\begin{itemize}
  \item[(i)] The number of states of a given multiplet is determined as follows:  
  Starting from the upper right (or lower left) corner, move to the adjacent state using the ladder operators  
  $\hat{T}_{-}$ ($\hat{T}_{+}$) or $\hat{V}_{-}$ ($\hat{V}_{+}$), respectively.  
  From there, use $\hat{T}_{-}$ ($\hat{T}_{+}$), $\hat{V}_{-}$ ($\hat{V}_{+}$), or $\hat{U}_{+}$ ($\hat{U}_{-}$)  
  to access the next possible state within the triangle. One cannot leave the triangle, but in this way,  
  one can reach all internal states. This procedure defines a lattice of states along and inside the triangle.  
  Counting all these states yields the total number of states in the multiplet.

  \item[(ii)] Counting along $T$-lines, the triplet $[3]$ consists of a $T$-singlet and a $T$-doublet;  
  counting along $V$-lines, a $V$-singlet and a $V$-doublet;  
  and along $U$-lines, a $U$-singlet and a $U$-doublet.  
  The same structure applies to the conjugate representation, the anti-triplet $[\overline{3}]$.

  \item[(iii)] The sextet $[6]$ includes a $T$-singlet, a $T$-doublet, and a $T$-triplet;  
  likewise, it contains a $V$- and a $U$-singlet, doublet, and triplet, respectively.  
  The same holds for the anti-sextet $[\overline{6}]$.

