  \item[(iv)] The decuplet $[10]$ contains a $T$-singlet, doublet, triplet, and quadruplet;  
  the same applies for $V$- and $U$-multiplets.  
  An analogous decomposition exists for the anti-decuplet $[\overline{10}]$.

  \item[(v)] The triplet $[3]$ with its three states corresponds to the fundamental representation of $(SU(3),\cdot)$.  
  In the next section, we will see that all other multiplets—including the singlet, the anti-triplet, and higher-dimensional ones—  
